      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "Herengracht, the first canal dug, was followed by Keizersgracht and then Prinsengracht, each named after a noble title.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        3,
        4
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.roughguides.com/netherlands/amsterdam/grachtengordel/",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "Expanding Amsterdam from two to seven square kilometres, the city reserved Herengracht, Keizersgracht and Prinsengracht for wealthy merchants, while cross‑streets housed artisans.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        5
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.roughguides.com/netherlands/amsterdam/grachtengordel/",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "The Grachtengordel was built in the 17th century as Amsterdam stretched beyond its medieval boundaries.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        7
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://amsterdamtourism.net/amsterdam-canal-belt/",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "Today, over 100 kilometres of canals wind through Amsterdam, lined by roughly 1,550 historic buildings still in use.",
